
  The good news is your answer already settles the earlier fork: it's a parameter, passed in, not something [38;5;153mlogin[39m
  returns. What it doesn't settle is what the identifier [3mis[0m — and that decides the whole shape of the thing.

  [1mThe fork:[0m an ID that already exists when the login form is submitted is a fundamentally different object from one the
  login establishes.

  - [1mA client/device ID, generated locally.[0m The app mints it on first visit, stores it, and every form reads it —
    including the login form, which is why it can be a parameter to [38;5;153mlogin[39m. It exists before anyone authenticates,
    survives logout, and is the same value for every form on the page. This is the reading that makes all three of your
    requirements true at once with no ordering problem.
  - [1mThe authenticated user's ID, from the backend.[0m Real identity, genuinely useful for "who logged in" — but it doesn't
    exist yet at the moment [38;5;153mlogin[39m is called, so it cannot be a parameter to [38;5;153mlogin[39m on the first attempt. It could be a
    parameter on [3mlater[0m calls (a stored ID from a previous session, used to pre-fill or re-authenticate), which is
    coherent but means [38;5;153mlogin[39m takes an ID that's absent the first time and present afterward.
  - [1mBoth, as two distinct fields.[0m A device ID that's always there plus a user ID that appears after the first successful
    login. Honest about the difference, and it's where apps usually end up — but it's two things to build and two
    lifetimes to reason about, and I'd rather not assume you want the second one yet.

  My recommendation is the first. It's the only one where "parameter on [38;5;153mlogin[39m", "works across the app", and "persists"
  are all straightforwardly true, and it's the smaller thing to build. If you actually want real user identity tracked,
  say so — that's the third option and it's a bigger design, but it's a legitimate thing to want and I'd rather build it
  deliberately than discover it halfway through.
